# ============================================
#  CURSOR PROJECT RULES — Indonesia Lip Reading (GNN)
#  FINAL (integrated with new requirements)
#  Last update: <commit-id or date when applied>
# ============================================

# OVERVIEW
# --------
# This file is the canonical project rulebook. It MUST be followed for any
# code, experiments, preprocessing, or results to be considered valid.
# Changes to these rules must be versioned and approved; add a Git commit
# message referencing the change. The goal is maximum reproducibility, low
# storage footprint, deterministic evaluation, and clean experiment artifacts.

# 0. GLOBAL PRINCIPLES
# --------------------
- Use PyTorch as the single ML framework. Store all feature/dataset artifacts in `.pt` format using `torch.save`.
- Use float32 for tensors (minimum required precision). Avoid float64.
- FPS is fixed to 25 for all preprocessing and feature calculations.
- Use only ONE seed for the canonical experiment runs: seed = 0. (Pilot runs may use other seeds locally but must be noted.)
- Use tmux for long-running scripts. Add small instructions in README for starting/stopping sessions.
- Keep code modular, single-responsibility, well-documented, and tested.
- All preprocessing is offline (extraction + FE) and saved. Models load precomputed artifacts.

# 1. REPOSITORY & DIRECTORY LAYOUT (MANDATORY)
# --------------------------------------------
/project
  /data
    raw/                              # original videos (read-only)
    IDLRW-DATASET/
      <word>/
        <split>/
          <video>.mp4
          <video>_meta.txt            # speech mask and meta (format below)
  /extracted                           # outputs from facemesh_extractor
    lips/
      <vidid>.pt                       # saved dict: { 'frames': n, 'landmarks': tensor, 'fps':25, 'meta': {} }
      example_checks/                   # generated images for node+adjacency checking
    mouth_area/
    full_face/
  /features
    B0/
      <vidid>.pt
    B1/
    B2/
    B3/
    B4/
  /graphs
    lips/
      adjacency.npy                     # numpy adjacency (saved for inspection), also saved in .pt artifact
      node_map.json
    mouth_area/
    full_face/
  /models
    checkpoints/                        # saved via torch.save (state_dict + config)
    configs/                            # *.yaml template configs (auto-tunable)
  /results
    <partition>/<FE>/<model>/<seed>/
      run_meta.pt                       # dict: {metrics, hyperparams, seed, git_hash, timestamps}
      metrics.pt                         # torch-saved dict of metrics per epoch
      classification_report.pt
      confusion_matrix.png
      gradcam/                           # PNGs per sample + aggregated PNGs
      loss_history.png
  /scripts
    run_extraction_all.sh
    run_models_all.sh
    generate_adjacency_check_images.sh
  /logs
    experiment_registry.pt              # master .pt dict that lists all runs and metadata
  README.md
  .cursorrules

# 2. DATASET AND METADATA FORMAT
# ------------------------------
- Dataset root: `IDLRW-DATASET/<words>/<splits>/<video>.mp4` with accompanying `<video>_meta.txt`.
- Required `video_meta.txt` structure (example content — these fields must exist):
  VidID: <int>
  VidName: <string>
  ChannelId: <string or empty>
  Start: <float_seconds>
  End: <float_seconds>
  Duration: <float_seconds>
  SpeechMask: <frame-wise binary mask or run-length encoding>  # required field

- SpeechMask specifics:
  - Because FPS=25, frames correspond to integer indices at 25 fps.
  - SpeechMask can be a compact textual format (e.g., "0-3,6,9-12") or a line with space-separated 0/1 values equal to number_of_frames.
  - The extractor must parse this file and include a 'speech_mask' tensor in the extracted `.pt` artifact for every video.

# 3. EXTRACTION RULES (facemesh_extractor.py)
# -------------------------------------------
- Single file: `facemesh_extractor.py`. Do NOT create duplicates.
- CLI args:
  --input-dir <raw dataset root>
  --partition {lips,mouth,full}
  --out-dir <project>/extracted/<partition>
  --fps 25
  --save-format pt
- Extraction pipeline:
  1. Load video at 25 FPS (resample if needed; log original fps in meta).
  2. Run MediaPipe FaceMesh inference on every frame.
  3. Apply per-frame filtering and smoothing (optional) deterministically.
  4. Select nodes according to partition:
     - full: use all 468 MediaPipe nodes and default adjacency topology.
     - mouth_area: explicit subset (mouth + cheeks + chin nodes) with adjacency preserved between remaining nodes.
     - lips: subset (inner+outer lips) with adjacency preserved among nodes.
  5. Build adjacency matrix for the chosen node set by pruning the full-face topology while preserving anatomical edges (edge if both endpoints exist).
  6. Node & adjacency CHECK:
     - Save one representative sample image per X videos (configurable) showing:
       - overlayed landmarks with node indices,
       - drawn edges from adjacency,
       - colored groups for AUs (if B4 grouping exists),
       - speech mask overlay (frames where speech=1 highlighted).
     - Save the image to `extracted/<partition>/example_checks/<vidid>_check.png`.
     - Any mismatches (e.g., adjacency referencing missing node ids) must produce an error and stop the extraction for that partition.
  7. Save `.pt` artifact per video in extracted/<partition>:
     torch.save(dict(
       vidid=int,
       vidname=str,
       fps=25,
       frames=int,
       landmarks=tensor(shape=(frames, n_nodes, 2), dtype=torch.float32),
       adjacency=tensor(shape=(n_nodes, n_nodes), dtype=torch.float32) OR saved adjacency separately,
       node_map=dict(face_idx -> node_idx),
       speech_mask=tensor(shape=(frames,), dtype=torch.uint8),
       meta=dict(start, end, duration, original_fps, extractor_version, git_hash)
     ), out_path)
  8. Extraction must be deterministic; set RNG seeds only for nondeterministic ops (if any) and log them.

# 4. ADJACENCY & NODE CHECKING
# ----------------------------
- After extraction for each partition:
  - Run `generate_adjacency_check_images.sh` which loads a random subset of extracted .pt artifacts and produces visual check images with:
    - landmarks overlaid,
    - adjacency edges drawn,
    - node indices visible,
    - AU-group colorings (if B4 AU mapping is defined),
    - speech mask frames marked.
- If any generated check image reveals disconnected nodes, duplicated indices, or mismatched adjacency references, the extractor must be fixed before running training.
- Save a short text summary `extracted/<partition>/example_checks/report.txt` listing any warnings or confirmations.

# 5. FEATURE ENGINEERING (B0–B4) — offline only
# ---------------------------------------------
- There are five FE levels:
  B0: raw normalized coords (per-node X,Y)
  B1: B0 + velocity (first derivative)
  B2: B1 + acceleration (second derivative) + speed magnitude
  B3: B2 + geometric features (pairwise distances for a small canonical set, angles, lip ratios)
  B4: B3 + AU-like grouped displacement features (Action Unit inspired) + PCA modes + motion-energy features

- Implementation rules:
  - Use the pre-saved `extracted/<partition>/*.pt` artifacts to compute all B*.
  - Save each resulting feature artifact to `/features/B*/<vidid>.pt` as a dict:
    { 'vidid', 'features': tensor(frames, n_nodes, n_features), 'meta': {...} }
  - All tensors must be float32.
  - For B4: include a mapping `au_nodes` describing which node indices contributed to each AU group (store this in meta).
- Normalization:
  - Perform normalization during FE computation and save normalization params (mean/std or min/max) into the feature file meta (so evaluation uses the same normalization).
  - Normalization strategy:
    - Per-video per-node normalization: center coordinates by face bounding box center, scale by inter-ocular distance (or other stable facial measure). Save scaling factor in meta.
    - Optionally, a global normalization (dataset mean/std) may be computed, but always save the parameters per-feature-set.
- Storage rule: only `.pt` features are allowed. Do not write CSV for features.

# 6. ACTION UNITS (AU) in B4
# --------------------------
- B4 must incorporate AU-inspired grouped features mindful of node selection:
  - Define AU groups (e.g., AU25 lips part, AU26 jaw drop, AU12 lip corner pull, AU27 mouth stretch) mapped to node index sets for each partition.
  - Compute group-level magnitudes per frame (e.g., mean absolute displacement for nodes in group).
  - Include AU group tensors into the per-frame feature vector and store AU->node mapping in feature meta.
- AU groups must be consistent across partitions (i.e., mapping defined in terms of MediaPipe base indices but pruned per partition via node_map).

# 7. DATA LOADING & NORMALIZATION RULES
# -------------------------------------
- Training loaders must:
  - Load `.pt` feature files and adjacency from `/features` and `/graphs`.
  - Apply only lightweight runtime augmentations (if any): e.g., dropout on node features, small temporal jitter (documented).
  - Use normalization params saved in feature meta — DO NOT recompute normalization at load time.
- No heavy FE at load time.

# 8. MODELS (allowed & forbidden)
# -------------------------------
- Allowed model types:
  SPATIO-ONLY: GCN, GAT, GraphSAGE, GIN
  TEMPORAL GNNS: ST-GCN, GConvLSTM
  HYBRID: GNN→LSTM, GNN→GRU, GNN→BiGRU, GNN→TemporalConv1D

- Forbidden:
  - Transformers (all variants)
  - TGAT, EvolveGCN (unless explicitly approved)
  - Any model that constructs edges at runtime

- All model code must accept adjacency and feature tensors as inputs, not construct adjacency.

# 9. TRAINING ENTRYPOINT (train.py) & CONFIGS
# -------------------------------------------
- Single entrypoint `train.py` MUST exist. CLI args:
  --partition {lips,mouth,full}
  --featureset {B0,B1,B2,B3,B4}
  --model <model_name>
  --config <path/to/config.yaml>
  --seed 0
  --out-dir <path>

- Config system:
  - Use YAML config templates stored under `/models/configs/`.
  - Config must include hyperparams (lr, batch_size, weight_decay), optimizer, scheduler, number of epochs, model-specific hyperparams, dataset paths, logging options, and early-stopping criteria.
  - Config must be auto-tunable friendly (keys clearly named, numeric ranges suggested in comments).

- Standardized training loop behaviors:
  - Load feature `.pt`, adjacency, speech_mask (if needed for masking loss).
  - Use deterministic dataloader settings for reproducibility (num_workers, pin_memory consistent).
  - Log:
    - epoch-level train/val loss, metrics
    - time per epoch
    - memory usage
    - best model checkpoint saved as `best.pth` (state_dict + optimizer state + config + seed + git_hash + timestamp)
    - final metrics saved as `.pt` dict and stored in results.
  - After completion, run evaluation on test split and save classification_report and confusion_matrix as `.pt` (and PNG for confusion matrix).

# 10. RUN SCRIPTS
# ----------------
- `scripts/run_extraction_all.sh`:
  - loops partitions (lips,mouth,full)
  - calls `facemesh_extractor.py` with seed=0, fps=25
  - after extraction for a partition, invokes adjacency image checks
- `scripts/run_models_all.sh`:
  - reads list of models + FE sets + partitions from a YAML grid file
  - launches `train.py` sequentially under tmux sessions or with GPU scheduling
  - default single-seed (0) execution
  - by checking availability of a file

# 11. EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN — INCREMENTAL EXPERIMENT PURPOSE (B0–B4)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
- B0 (Baseline): Can geometrical raw landmark coordinates alone separate visemes? Purpose: baseline spatial information.
- B1 (Velocity): Tests whether dynamic motion (first derivative) improves separability beyond B0.
- B2 (Accel + speed): Tests whether acceleration and speed magnitude add additional discriminative power (temporal dynamics).
- B3 (Geometry): Tests whether explicit pairwise geometric relations (distances/angles/ratios) reduce viseme confusion that coords alone can't handle.
- B4 (Full + AU): Tests if grouping into AU-like features and PCA motion modes captures higher-level articulatory patterns and improves robustness and interpretability.
- Experimental rule: run full grid across partitions × B0..B4 × allowed models with seed=0 as canonical. Use pilot results to shortlist a small set for deeper analysis if necessary (but keep seed=0 as canonical for reproducibility/tracking).

# 12. METRICS, LOGGING & RESULT STORAGE (NEATNESS)
# ------------------------------------------------
- All logs and metrics must be saved as `.pt` (torch-saved dicts) under `/results/<partition>/<FE>/<model>/<seed>/`.
- Example run_meta.pt structure:
  {
    'vid': <id>,
    'partition': 'lips',
    'featureset': 'B2',
    'model': 'gcn',
    'seed': 0,
    'git_hash': <commit>,
    'hyperparams': {...},
    'metrics': { 'best_val_acc': x, 'test_acc': y, 'f1_macro': z },
    'time': { 'start': ts, 'end': ts, 'epoch_times': [...] },
    'notes': '...'
  }
- Also save:
  - confusion_matrix.png
  - gradcam aggregated PNGs per-class
  - loss_history.png
- Maintain master experiment registry at `/logs/experiment_registry.pt` which is a dict of all runs and pointers to result folders. Update atomically after each finished run.

# 13. TABLES / VISUALIZATIONS (per-model)
# ---------------------------------------
- For each model run:
  - Generate and save:
    - metrics.pt (per-epoch dict)
    - classification_report.pt (per-class metrics)
    - confusion_matrix.png
    - gradcam/ PNGs (sample-level and aggregated)
  - Additionally: create a compact per-run summary table (dictionary) and save as `.pt` for easy loading. Do not save CSVs for the canonical artifacts; saving a human-readable CSV is optional but discouraged. Preferred: if a tabular human-readable summary is needed, convert `.pt` to a one-off CSV via a utility script but keep canonical storage in `.pt`.

# 14. DOCUMENTATION OF TRAIN RUNS
# -------------------------------
- Every run must produce a README-like `run_readme.txt` in the run folder summarizing:
  - dataset version & path
  - FE version & path
  - adjacency path
  - seed
  - git commit hash
  - config file path & contents
  - quick human-readable result summary
- This file must be automatically generated by `train.py` at the end of each run.

# 15. REPRODUCIBILITY & MODULARITY
# --------------------------------
- All code must be modular:
  - extractor module
  - fe module
  - graph utils module
  - model modules
  - training loop module
  - utils (logging, plotting)
- Tests: minimal unit tests for adjacency builder and feature computation must exist (can be simple smoke tests) and be run locally before large runs.

# 16. TMUX & LONG-RUN INSTRUCTIONS
# --------------------------------
- Add small helper scripts in `/scripts` showing:
  - `tmux new -s lipread` start
  - `tmux attach -t lipread` attach
  - `tmux kill-session -t lipread` stop
- `run_models_all.sh` must be designed to be started inside a tmux session.

# 17. STORAGE & SIZE RULES
# ------------------------
- Feature files (.pt) must be minimal (float32). Avoid duplication across partitions (do not store identical tensors for repeated content).
- Model checkpoints can be pruned to only keep `best.pth` and optionally last.pth; older epoch checkpoints not required.
- The experiment registry must be compact and not store entire tensors — only metadata and paths.

# 18. ACKNOWLEDGE DATA LAYOUT (for cursor)
# ----------------------------------------
- Cursor (CI / automation) must be informed of data layout:
  IDLRW-DATASET/<words>/<splits>/<video>.mp4 & <video_meta>.txt
- Cursor must verify that each video_meta contains the fields in Section 2 and parse the SpeechMask into a frame-wise tensor matching FPS=25.
- Example meta that must be parsed:
  VidID: 0
  VidName: matanajwa20212_100_125_4.mp4
  ChannelId:
  Start: 0.12 End: 0.88
  Duration: 0.76 seconds
  SpeechMask: 0 0 1 1 1 0 0  (or equivalent ranges)

# 19. YAML CONFIG TEMPLATE & AUTO-TUNING
# --------------------------------------
- Provide one YAML template `models/configs/template.yaml` with placeholders and default ranges for:
  - learning_rate: 1e-3
  - batch_size: 32
  - weight_decay: 1e-4
  - model-specific params (hidden_dim, num_layers, dropout)
  - training params (epochs, early_stop_patience)
- Configs must be loadable and validated by the training script.
- Optionally add comments in the YAML to guide auto-tuner tools (e.g., Optuna) for future tuning but do not require tuning by default.

# 20. FINAL EXPERIMENT RULE (single-seed canonical)
# -------------------------------------------------
- The canonical published runs use seed=0 across the full grid (all partitions × B0..B4 × allowed models).
- If additional seeds are needed for robustness, they must be run only for a small shortlisted set, and documented as non-canonical extensions.

# 21. MAINTENANCE, UPDATES & APPROVAL
# -----------------------------------
- Any change to `.cursorrules` must be proposed via a PR and committed with a short justification.
- The `extractor_version` and `git_hash` must be recorded in every saved artifact.

# 22. FAILURE & RECOVERY
# ----------------------
- If an extraction run fails, the extractor must leave a `.failed` marker with logs and the failing frame index.
- `run_extraction_all.sh` should skip `.failed` and continue but record failures into `extracted/<partition>/example_checks/report.txt`.

# 23. QUICK CHECKLIST BEFORE LARGE RUN
# -----------------------------------
- [ ] dataset present and validated (cursor parse)
- [ ] adjacency check images generated and validated
- [ ] all partitions extracted without errors
- [ ] features (B0..B4) computed and normalized; normalization meta saved
- [ ] YAML configs prepared
- [ ] tmux session available
- [ ] disk capacity >= recommended size
- [ ] experiment_registry accessible
- [ ] seed set to 0 in scripts

# 24. CONTACT / NOTES
# -------------------
- Add author and contact info in the README and within .cursorrules header when finalizing.

